tor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"cmd-31012eb80c5f4169\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a LUKS2-encrypted XFS volume at /srv/secure on node1 and wired it into systemd (loop-attach service, crypttab, cryptsetup override, fstab) so it unlocks and mounts automatically at boot. Both material outcomes are directly verified: the volume itself (create/format/mount/data integrity) and, critically, the automatic boot-time behavior, which was validated not just by pre-reboot simulation but by an actual systemctl reboot with boot_id changing (15443601... -> fa9e5fdb...) and full post-reboot confirmation that the mount, crypto mapping, and data survived without manual steps.\",\"limitations\":[\"A handful of transient log/marker files were left under /tmp after the run; low severity and not evaluated as task-blocking.\",\"The design pins the loop device to a fixed /dev/loop0 node, which is a reasonable choice in this single-node test context but was not stress-tested against contention from other loop consumers across multiple reboots.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Provide an encrypted volume mounted at /srv/secure on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"A LUKS2-encrypted (aes-xts-plain64) volume backed by a loop-mounted sparse image was created, formatted XFS, and mounted at /srv/secure; a test file was written and read back successfully both immediately after creation and after a real reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9bccf650140f45d0","cmd-31012eb80c5f4169","cmd-583b4169eec543d6"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Volume unlocks and mounts on its own at boot (no manual intervention)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Boot-time wiring (secure-loop-setup.service -> systemd-cryptsetup@secure.service -> srv-secure.mount, pulled in via cryptsetup.target/local-fs.target, crypttab and fstab entries, unit enabled) was configured, then validated via a real node restart: pre-restart boot_id 154436